    ('v10.0.0', 'Flying Carpet 10.0.0', 'The Android version is available on the [Play Store](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=dev.spiegl.flyingcarpet), or an APK is available here if you would like to sideload. It supports Android 10/SDK 29 and up.

The iOS version is available [here](https://apps.apple.com/us/app/flying-carpet-file-transfer/id1637377410) or search the App Store for "Flying Carpet File Transfer".

## Flying Carpet 10.0

**10.0.1**: Windows only. Fixes the firewall rule check failing on non-English systems and non-ASCII install paths, which caused a UAC prompt on every transfer.
**10.0.2**: Android only. Fixes a missing local network access permission check that is newly required in Android 17 (SDK 37).
**10.0.3**: Android only. Fixes shared network transfers failing to connect when a VPN is running. Also fixes a crash when Bluetooth is turned off during a transfer.
**10.0.4**: iOS and macOS only. Fixes memory use growing with the size of the transfer on the receiving device, which crashed iOS partway through large transfers. Also fixes the password dialog being cut off on iPad.
**10.0.5**: Linux only. Compiles with newer version of tauri-cli to fix an issue on Fedora.

Version 10 adds Shared Network mode and rebuilds the encryption on the Noise Protocol Framework.

Version 10 is a breaking change. Version 10 devices can''t transfer with version 9 or earlier.

### Shared Network mode

Flying Carpet formerly always created its own WiFi hotspot. Now you can transfer over a WiFi or wired network both devices are already on, which is faster to start when there''s a network handy.

The receiving device generates a one-time password and shows it with a QR code; type or scan it on the sending device.

This allows Apple-to-Apple transfers to work. It also unblocks Android-to-iOS (#131), lets a desktop on Ethernet transfer with a phone on WiFi (#124), and works on machines without WiFi (#93). Machines with several network interfaces get a dropdown showing each one''s IP.

### Rebuilt encryption

Every transfer, in both modes, now runs a Noise handshake (X25519, ChaCha20-Poly1305, SHA-256), with the key derived from your password by PBKDF2 at 600,000 iterations. Full writeup in docs/shared-network-crypto.md.

### macOS and Linux Bluetooth

Hotspot transfers between a Mac and a Linux machine used to require pairing the two in System Settings first, and often failed afterward with "Peer removed pairing information". Linux now bonds over an LE socket, which stops it reaching macOS over classic Bluetooth, where macOS serves no GATT. Pairing also shows the 6-digit code for you to confirm.

### Send Folder

Sending a folder now recreates that folder on the receiving device with the contents inside, on all five platforms. Previously only macOS did this — everywhere else the files were dumped loose into the destination. Sending individual files is unchanged.', '2026-07-26T23:57:28Z'::timestamptz, false, 'android_FlyingCarpet_10.0.3.apk', 'https://github.com/spieglt/FlyingCarpet/releases/download/v10.0.0/android_FlyingCarpet_10.0.3.apk', 15242731::bigint, '[{"name":"android_FlyingCarpet_10.0.2.apk","url":"https://github.com/spieglt/FlyingCarpet/releases/download/v10.0.0/android_FlyingCarpet_10.0.2.apk","size":15236259},{"name":"android_FlyingCarpet_10.0.3.apk","url":"https://github.com/spieglt/FlyingCarpet/releases/download/v10.0.0/android_FlyingCarpet_10.0.3.apk","size":15242731}]'::jsonb),
